---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import { aiEngineeringCaseStudy } from '../../data/case-studies';
import '../../styles/case-ai.css';

const sectionLinks = [
    { href: '#incident', label: 'Incident' },
    { href: '#workflow', label: 'Workflow' },
    { href: '#outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
    study={aiEngineeringCaseStudy}
    sectionLinks={sectionLinks}
>
    <Fragment slot="intro">
        I used OpenCode and Claude Code while rewriting Paper Trails from Laravel to Next.js. A customer
        completed a free checkout, but the application didn't create an order. I traced the cause, wrote the
        plan and used the agents to draft the code and tests. I reviewed and simplified the result before
        deployment.
    </Fragment>
    <dl slot="facts" class="facts">
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>
                Role
            </dt>
            <dd>
                Technical Director and Co-Founder
            </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>
                System
            </dt>
            <dd>
                Paper Trails checkout: Next.js / Stripe / MongoDB
            </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>
                AI tools
            </dt>
            <dd>
                Claude Code and OpenCode
            </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>
                Result
            </dt>
            <dd>
                No repeat failures observed from January through July 2026
            </dd>
        </div>
    </dl>
    <section class="section" id="incident">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">
                01 / Diagnosis
            </p>
            <h2>
                The incident
            </h2>
            <div class="incident">
                <article class="incident__item">
                    <span class="incident__label">
                        The report
                    </span>
                    <p>
                        A customer completed checkout with a 100% discount, but Paper Trails never created
                        the order.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="incident__item">
                    <span class="incident__label">
                        The cause
                    </span>
                    <p>
                        Stripe doesn't create a PaymentIntent when the checkout total is zero. The
                        application had stored the order details only on that payment record.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="incident__item">
                    <span class="incident__label">
                        The fix
                    </span>
                    <p>
                        I moved the order details to the Stripe Checkout Session, which exists for both paid
                        and free orders.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
            <p class="caption">
                I checked the Paper Trails logs and Stripe records to confirm the cause. Then I wrote a plan
                covering the change, the files involved and the checks I needed before deployment.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="workflow">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">
                02 / Implementation
            </p>
            <h2>
                From diagnosis to release
            </h2>
            <div class="pipeline" role="img" aria-label="Checkout fix from the customer report through my diagnosis and plan, AI drafting, my review, testing, release and monitoring">
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        01
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        Customer report
                    </strong>
                </div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    -&gt;
                </span>
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        02
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        I diagnose the problem
                    </strong>
                </div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    -&gt;
                </span>
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        03
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        I write the plan
                    </strong>
                </div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    -&gt;
                </span>
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        04
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        AI drafts code and tests
                    </strong>
                </div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    -&gt;
                </span>
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        05
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        I review and simplify
                    </strong>
                </div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    -&gt;
                </span>
                <div class="step">
                    <span class="step__number">
                        06
                    </span>
                    <strong>
                        Test, release and monitor
                    </strong>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="caption">
                The agents worked from that plan. I reviewed their implementation and test coverage,
                simplified the code, then checked the fix before releasing it and monitoring the result.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">
                03 / Review
            </p>
            <h2>
                Implementation and review
            </h2>
            <div class="cards">
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>
                        What I decided
                    </h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            Confirm how Stripe handles a checkout with a zero total.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Use the Checkout Session as the source of order details.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Require a unique Stripe checkout session ID in the database to prevent duplicate
                            orders.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Choose the automated and manual checks needed before deployment.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Add a browser test as follow-up protection.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </article>
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>
                        What the agents drafted
                    </h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            Production code based on my written plan.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Focused automated tests for payments and orders.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Code and security reviews against the agreed design.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
                <p>
                    During review I removed two browser-side checks that added complexity. The
                    database's unique Stripe session ID was the protection against duplicate orders. I
                    kept the browser code straightforward and left the larger checkout redesign for a
                    separate follow-up.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="outcome">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">
                04 / Verification
            </p>
            <h2>
                Checks and outcome
            </h2>
            <div class="result">
                <div class="result__primary">
                    <strong>
                        No repeat failures observed
                    </strong>
                    <p>
                        I saw no repeat failures between the January 2026 deployment and July 2026.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="result__supporting">
                    <h3>
                        Checks before deployment
                    </h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            Unit tests passed.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Code quality, formatting and production build checks passed.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            I completed a free checkout manually in Stripe's test environment.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The order was saved and matched to its Stripe checkout session.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The affected customer confirmed successful completion.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="cards cards--spaced">
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>
                        The later browser test
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                        I added the Playwright browser test after the fix went live, alongside a later
                        checkout redesign. That follow-up moved thank-you page processing from the browser
                        to the server. The browser test wasn't one of the checks I ran before the
                        initial deployment.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>
                        What I took from it
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                        The written plan gave the agents a clear change to work on and gave me something
                        concrete to review against. I still needed to check the implementation and remove
                        unnecessary code. I haven't measured a speed or defect-rate improvement for
                        this incident.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">
                05 / Project records
            </p>
            <h2>
                What I checked
            </h2>
            <ul class="evidence-list">
                <li>
                    My incident plan, including the cause, chosen solution, files to change and completed
                    checks.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Project history covering the initial fix, my simplification and the later redesign.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Automated tests for payments, orders and Stripe settings.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The later Playwright test for a digital checkout with a zero total.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Automated checks for formatting, code quality, unit tests, production builds and browser
                    tests.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="caption">
                Paper Trails is named with permission. I've left out customer data, order details,
                logs, credentials, environment settings and private repository links.
            </p>
            <div class="cta">
                <a class="button button--primary" href="/">
                    Back to portfolio
                </a>
                <a class="button" href="/Adam-Brewer-Senior-Full-Stack-Engineer-CV.pdf" download>
                    Download CV
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
